  "julia_one_ulp_mitigation": "this engine drops |c| <= eps, PauliPropagation.jl drops |c| < eps (benchmarks/julia/README.md \u00a7P3). Rust runs use the dyadic verbatim; jl runs get math.nextafter(eps, inf), which makes jl's rule exactly this engine's. Both numbers are recorded per parity case.",
  "kim2023_provenance": {
    "source": "Y. Kim, A. Eddins, S. Anand, K. X. Wei, E. van den Berg, S. Rosenblatt, H. Nayfeh, Y. Wu, M. Zaletel, K. Temme, A. Kandala, \"Evidence for the utility of quantum computing before fault tolerance\", Nature 618, 500-505 (2023)",
    "doi": "10.1038/s41586-023-06096-3",
    "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt6bf3w13h/qt6bf3w13h.pdf",
    "supplementary_url": "https://static-content.springer.com/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41586-023-06096-3/MediaObjects/41586_2023_6096_MOESM1_ESM.pdf",
    "checked": "The published-version PDF (UC eScholarship mirror of the Nature article; nature.com itself requires cookie auth and refuses automated fetch) and the Supplementary Information PDF were both fetched and text-extracted. The operator supports are printed as the Fig. 3 / Fig. 4 panel titles, e.g. Fig. 3b's title is the literal string \"<X13,29,31 Y9,30 Z8,12,17,28,32>\"; the per-observable `source_detail` below quotes the panel title it was read from. The main text and PubMed Central copy (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10266970/) were cross-read for the accompanying definitions and Clifford eigenvalues.",
    "corroborating_sources": [
      "T. Begusic, J. Gray, G. K.-L. Chan, Sci. Adv. (arXiv:2308.05077), Fig. 3 caption, which expands all four panel labels verbatim and so disambiguates the two distinct weight-17 operators",
      "T. Begusic, G. K.-L. Chan, arXiv:2306.16372, Fig. 1 panel titles",
      "J. Tindall, M. Fishman, E. M. Stoudenmire, D. Sels, PRX Quantum 5, 010308 (2024) (arXiv:2306.14887), Eq. (4), which also states the Z_13 stabilizer origin",
      "Y. Liao, D. Wang, Z. Zhou, X. Zhang, Z.-Y. Xiang, arXiv:2308.03082"
    ],
    "retrieved": "2026-08-31",
    "qubit_indexing": "IBM Eagle device numbering 0..126. The experiment ran on ibm_kyiv; this repo's lattice comes from the ibm_sherbrooke (FakeSherbrooke) coupling map. No fetched source asserts the two index maps are identical, but the `verification` note below closes that gap computationally: the published supports are reproduced exactly on the Sherbrooke map, including the weight-17 operator whose 5-step causal cone covers 68 qubits.",
    "verification": "Every entry with a `stabilizer_origin` is reproduced by this repo's own engine, not merely transcribed: propagating the seed observable forward through `circuits.heavy_hex_kicked_ising(127, trotter_steps, theta_h=pi/2, order='x-then-zz')` yields a single Pauli string whose support and sign equal the `support` and `clifford_eigenvalue` recorded here. This is checked by `test_published_supports_are_reproduced_by_the_stabilizer_relation` in `python/paulistrings/tests/test_examples_circuits.py`. It simultaneously validates the support transcription, the heavy-hex edge list, the Trotter layer order, and the ibm_kyiv/ibm_sherbrooke index correspondence."
  },
